+# Earlier profiles: controller navigation during startup
+
+Earlier profiles keeps **Back to game** and **Reload recovery** usable while release information is loading, timed out or failed. The shared controller navigation adapter starts independently of the version/catalog request. One router owns the page through retries and readiness; retrying does not require a fresh controller join.
+
+Reload replaces the current startup read, cancels its deadline and rejects its late result. It does not discover profiles or invoke any profile repair/write. After a successful startup, Reload is hidden and the ordinary explicit Find/Review workflow remains. If Reload is the focused action as it retires, focus moves to Find only while that same startup is current and the page remains foreground. Newer focus and background completion prevent this handoff; foreground return does not schedule another focus request.
+
+Back invalidates the startup generation and stops navigation immediately before leaving. Once data controls are available, the existing view still closes its read-only reader before Back leaves. A hidden/unfocused page does not consume controller input; a held command cannot become a new action on return. Cached departure stops sampling and closes the current read/view; a persisted return restarts the existing read lifecycle with one navigation loop. Terminal departure destroys the navigation owner and cannot be revived by a late response or pageshow.
+
+This correction composes after [shared Recovery reading preferences](recovery-display-readability.md), which changes only presentation. It does not add restore/migration, automatic Find, profile writes, new reader capabilities or device-specific controls.
+
+## Source candidate and verification
+
+The additive proposal is based on `a13ab970222498d7c5fa7f62f9fc04fe436979d5` plus the separately retained P05 reading patch. That earlier patch and its results remain immutable. The navigation proposal changes the Recovery host, adds Reload markup, adds eight actual-entry tests and updates two existing expectations that loading/error now retains a navigation loop and Back completes owned cleanup.
+
+The six complete affected files pass **76/76 on Node 20.19.5 and 76/76 on Node 22.22.2**, sequentially with a 1024 MiB V8 old-space cap. All 95 explicit inputs / 1,139,341 bytes remained unchanged. The eight new cases fail with the original host runtime and the same new Reload markup; this discriminator isolates missing navigation/ownership behavior, not merely a missing button. Original logs and exact input manifests are retained in the preparation packet. Changed JavaScript syntax and formatting pass.
+
+Those tests use the actual page/application entry, the real router and navigation adapter, a modeled standard controller, held build/catalog reads and the existing Recovery reader/view lifecycle. They prove zero automatic profile inspection, one live router/poll loop, controller Back/Reload before readiness, late-read refusal, current-focus retirement, neutral input after backgrounding and terminal cleanup. They do not establish a real controller, browser focus painting, browser cache admission, native file download or full P03/P05 acceptance.
+
+## Native acceptance journey
+
+In the served composed candidate, hold build information and reach Back/Reload without a mouse. Retry once, deliver the old response late, and confirm only the replacement becomes ready. Fail/timeout startup, retry with the same physical controller and continue into the existing Find/Review menu. Complete startup while Reload is focused, while Back has newer focus and while the page is backgrounded; inspect the actual focus ring and next action. Return from a cached/ordinary history traversal without accidental actions, then explicitly leave. Repeat Theme/Plain and Large reading settings with portrait/short-landscape layout and real touch/controller separately.
+
+Maintainer prompt: “Make startup escape actions navigable before data is ready using one page-owned router. Preserve physical edge history across explicit Reload, cancel only the replaced read/deadline, and never auto-discover profiles. Retire focused Reload to Find only under current foreground ownership; do not defer focus to a later return. Keep the existing read-only reader's Back/Close guarantees and retain actual failed/held/late-response receipts.”
